20. Pharmacovigilance Interpretation

The analysis identifies several reports that may warrant expert review based on severity and outcome.

However, this dataset does not support a conclusion of causality or disproportionality.
Important limitations include:

Only 8 reports are available.
Each suspected drug appears once.
Each drug–event combination appears once.
No denominator data are available.
There is no exposure data.
There is no comparator group.
There is no background incidence rate.
There is no reporting-database disproportionality analysis.
The dataset does not contain a dedicated seriousness field.
The data are synthetic/de-identified for analytical practice.

Therefore, the findings should be interpreted as descriptive signal exploration only.
21. Signal-Detection Limitations

A higher reporting frequency does not automatically establish a safety signal.

Proper signal assessment may require additional information such as:

Number of exposed patients
Reporting rate
Background incidence
Time trends
Concomitant medications
Patient medical history
Alternative causes
Dechallenge/rechallenge information
Dose and duration
Population characteristics
Literature and clinical evidence
Larger safety databases

The present project does not perform a formal causal assessment.

22. Data Quality Considerations

The source dataset is a small synthetic aggregate dataset.

Before using a similar dataset for real-world pharmacovigilance analysis, the following quality checks should be performed:
Missing values
Duplicate reports
Duplicate cases
Inconsistent drug names
Inconsistent reaction terminology
Invalid ages
Invalid severity categories
Invalid outcome categories
Date consistency
Reporter-type consistency
Coding-standard consistency

For real-world pharmacovigilance work, standardized terminology and validated data-processing procedures would also be required.
