24. Final Conclusion

This adverse-event signal exploration analyzed eight de-identified reports to identify reporting patterns and potential cases requiring expert review.

The dataset contains eight different suspected drugs and eight different drug–event combinations, with each combination occurring once. Therefore, no repeated high-frequency drug–event combination was
identified.

Three reports were categorized as Severe or Life-Threatening, representing 37.5% of the dataset. Three reports had a Hospitalized outcome, also representing 37.5%.

The Warfarin–Uncontrolled Bleeding report was the only Life-Threatening case and had a Hospitalized outcome. Ciprofloxacin–Tendonitis and Ibuprofen–Gastric Ulcer were classified as Severe and also had Hospitalized outcomes.
These cases can be prioritized for further expert review based on their reported severity and outcomes.

However, the analysis does not establish causality and does not constitute a formal pharmacovigilance signal confirmation. The small dataset, absence of exposure denominators, absence of comparator data, and lack of repeated drug–event combinations substantially limit interpretation.

The project therefore demonstrates a descriptive, non-causal adverse-event signal exploration workflow rather than a definitive safety assessment.
